Z||000003||Galen||06-20-2001||05:12 PM||||Any car can catch on fiero. I mean fire. (that was a error, but it looked cool so I am leaving it). Its just the basics with using electricty, gas and oil. I mean come on ya'll.. you try sticking a sparkie plug(champion or not) in a gastank and it'll go kaboom. Even in them precious vettes, stangs and 'maro's.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/devil.gif">       <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>    -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||88CoupeV6||06-20-2001||05:16 PM||||The problem was with the 84's mostly. Some had defective connecting rods that would break and slam through the side of the block. It would allow hot oil to touch the Cat and flame up. As long as you keep your oil full and Check for leaves or other things in the engine area you will be fine. They don't have any chance of catching fire more than any other type of car.<P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||4
Z||000005||Philphine||06-20-2001||05:23 PM||||what 88 said. even if it is an '84, unless it's got unusually low miles the connecting rod problem should have shown up by now.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tigger||06-20-2001||11:44 PM||||Another thing to look out for is leaky valve covers, but that's an easy fix.||||reg||1
